course: dictionary
lesson: 10868
title: Victory and winning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Victory means winning. You can win a game, a race, a contest, a battle or a war, and that win is a victory. In «George Washington led the American army to victory», victory means his army won the fight.
explain: Victory can also mean success in a struggle against something difficult, not just in a game. If you work hard and beat a problem that felt impossible, you have won a victory over it. Look at the words around victory: led, army, battle, win and struggle are all clues.
explain: Victory is a noun, so it can be the name of a thing or an event. You can say «a victory» or «victories», and you can talk about «victory» on its own. Phrases like «a narrow victory» (a win by a small amount) and «a victory lap» (a run to celebrate a win) help you use the word well.
fact: Victory means winning a game, race, contest, battle or war.
fact: Victory can also mean success in a struggle against something difficult.
fact: «A narrow victory» means winning by only a small amount.
fact: Joan of Arc, a teenage girl, led the French to victory at Orléans.
fact: Check your meaning by putting «win» in the place of victory: if it still makes sense, you are on the right track.
exercise: «George Washington led the American army to victory.» What does victory mean here? | A dinner party | *Winning a war | Losing a battle | A long walk home
exercise: Tom says victory means losing a race. What does victory really mean? | Breaking a rule in a game | *Winning a race | Running in the rain | Losing a race at the last second
exercise: Ella's team had a narrow victory. What does narrow victory mean? | A game where nobody wins | A race run on a narrow path | *A win by only a small amount | A win by a huge score
test: After months of practice, Sam finally beat his fear of swimming. What kind of victory is this? | *A victory over something difficult | A holiday at the seaside | A mistake in a game | A defeat in a race
test: What is a victory? | *Winning a game, race, contest, battle or war | Breaking a rule on purpose | Playing a game that nobody wins | Losing a race in the last second
exam: Sam says victory is only for big battles in history. Is he right? | Yes, victory is only for history lessons | No, victory means losing a game | Yes, victory is only for wars | *No, victory can also mean winning a game or race, or succeeding against something difficult
